��i<?php exit; ?>a:2:{s:7:"content";O:8:"stdClass":24:{s:2:"ID";i:5349;s:11:"post_author";s:1:"1";s:9:"post_date";s:19:"2026-04-01 09:57:01";s:13:"post_date_gmt";s:19:"2026-04-01 09:57:01";s:12:"post_content";s:762:"<p>Start with one environment, strong RBAC, and a single paved path for deploys. Fancy service meshes can wait until someone can explain the last outage.</p><ul class="blog-details__list"><li>Automate upgrades on a schedule</li><li>Keep staging honest, not a toy</li><li>Document the happy path in one page</li></ul><p>Kubernetes is not “install and chill” for teams under ten people. Without guardrails, you inherit YAML archaeology and clusters that nobody fully understands.</p><blockquote class="blog-details__blockquote"><p>Culture is what people do when nobody is watching the dashboard—how they escalate, how they admit uncertainty, and how they ship fixes.</p></blockquote><h2 class="blog-details__h2">How teams reduce risk while moving faster</h2>";s:10:"post_title";s:37:"Kubernetes Operations for Small Teams";s:12:"post_excerpt";s:285:"Kubernetes Operations for Small Teams — Kubernetes is not “install and chill” for teams under ten people. Without guardrails, you inherit YAML archaeology and clusters that nobody fully understands. Start with one environment, strong RBAC, and a single paved path for deploys.…";s:11:"post_status";s:7:"publish";s:14:"comment_status";s:4:"open";s:11:"ping_status";s:4:"open";s:13:"post_password";s:0:"";s:9:"post_name";s:37:"kubernetes-operations-for-small-teams";s:7:"to_ping";s:0:"";s:6:"pinged";s:0:"";s:13:"post_modified";s:19:"2026-04-01 09:57:01";s:17:"post_modified_gmt";s:19:"2026-04-01 09:57:01";s:21:"post_content_filtered";s:0:"";s:11:"post_parent";i:0;s:4:"guid";s:55:"https://nsc.test/kubernetes-operations-for-small-teams/";s:10:"menu_order";i:0;s:9:"post_type";s:4:"post";s:14:"post_mime_type";s:0:"";s:13:"comment_count";s:1:"0";s:6:"filter";s:3:"raw";}s:15:"key_version_all";i:1;}